        rules: {
          objective: 'Correctly identify which of the three face-down cards is the queen (or money card). In honest play between friends, the punter wins the stake back plus the same amount again for a correct guess; in the street scam the dealer uses sleight of hand so the punter loses.',
          gameplay: '1. The dealer shows three cards (one queen, two others—often two black jacks).\n2. The dealer throws the three cards face-down on the table and mixes them quickly.\n3. The punter bets on which face-down card is the queen.\n4. If correct (in honest play), the punter receives the stake back plus the same amount again; otherwise the stake is lost. In the scam, the dealer ensures the punter never wins.',
          keyRules: [
            'Only three cards are used: one target card (usually a queen) and two others.',
            'The dealer mixes the cards face-down; the punter must track or guess the target card.',
            'Bet is placed on one of the three positions before the chosen card is revealed.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Do not play for money in street settings—it is a scam; the dealer uses sleight of hand and accomplices (shills) so the punter cannot win.',
          intermediate: 'If playing honestly with friends with no real money, the only skill is visual tracking of the queen during the mix; the dealer can make this very difficult.',
          advanced: 'Sources advise: play only with no real money involved and explain the trick afterwards. In a true monte scam the mark has no chance of winning.',
          tips: [
            'Do not be misled by apparent winners—they are often shills.',
            'Play only for fun with friends and no money at stake.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
